he reserve and staging into the whole verb. It runs the attempt's last renewal first, with a lease covering the commit's transaction deadline, and sends no renewal after it. It then reads the session's `psum:` summaries and refuses `EntityTooLarge` a part that would take the session past its staged-chunk ceiling, as it refuses one whose staging stopped at the per-part chunk bound, compensating what was staged. That read is a check, not a fence: commits of different parts racing it can overshoot by at most the in-flight cap times the per-part bound, which the reference budget already charges. The commit is one batch: the session required `Open` on the bytes the attempt holds, the part key required absent (or, for a re-upload, at the prior record's bytes), the attempt's own slot required at the bytes it last wrote; the part record and the summary derived from it put together; the slot and every owned entry deleted; and, for a re-upload over a non-empty part, one `retire:bytes:` obligation naming the replaced part's chunks. A commit that answers anything but `Committed` is judged from a re-read, never guessed. It counts as the attempt's own only when the attempt's slot is gone (absent, or reserved by another attempt) and the session read after that is still the epoch the commit required, which no other writer and no teardown can produce; never on the strength of the part's bytes, since an empty part names no chunk. While a commit may still land, the attempt only retries it under the same slot bytes, so at most one lands, and if its rounds run out it ends in an error classed indeterminate. Once the commit is known not to have landed and unable to land later, a same-number rival that won, or a Complete that rolled the session back to a newer `Open` epoch, is answered `OperationAborted` after one compensation batch under the session's current epoch that releases the slot, deletes the owned entries and installs one obligation naming the attempt's own chunks (none for an empty part: an obligation must name a chunk); a session no longer `Open` is answered `NoSuchUpload`, its residue left to the reaper. Every loop of the commit and the compensation has a stated bound. Nothing calls the commit in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
 
 The post-restore reconciliation keeps one more singleton beside them, `mpufence`: the **restore-fence generation** that proposal 0016 requires to complete before any gateway serves multipart verbs on a restored image (0016 names the generation but gives it no key). Its value is `{"generation":N,"complete":B}`, closed and canonical like `mpuctl`'s. `generation` numbers that pass's runs from 1, so the record is absent until the first run, and absent is the only spelling of "no run has happened". `complete` says whether that run finished its session fence. Each run first writes the next generation as not complete, and marks or fences nothing until that write is acknowledged. After its last write it records the generation complete, but only if every write it made was acknowledged and no upload session is left for a human: one it could not read or fence, or a fenced one whose segment records need a human. Every run judges those sessions afresh from their records, so a session an earlier run fenced but could not clear holds back every later run until someone repairs it. Findings that are not about a session, such as a lost or misplaced chunk or an unreadable object or staged record, do not hold the generation back. Both writes are conditioned on the bytes last read there, so a completion that lands late never overwrites a newer run's generation. What the record cannot tell: a restore rewinds it with everything else, so an image captured after an earlier run completed comes back reading complete until the next run starts. No gateway reads it yet. The gateway gate (#508) needs a restore-scoped signal beside it, and until then what keeps multipart off a restored image is the runbook's order: the reconciliation runs with writers stopped, before any gateway is re-enabled.
 
